// The editor's Run-test control.
//
// ## Why this file exists at all
//
// The control has been in the product since `makeTestAction`
// (`ui/editor.nim`) was written and **it had no stylesheet**. Its markup asks
// for `flow-parallel flow-parallel-value-single editor-test-action` inside
// `flow-loop-step-container` inside `flow-content-widget`, and only the first
// three of those have rules — all of them belonging to the FLOW pane, which
// paints loop iteration chips. So the button inherited a loop chip's
// background, a loop chip's border radius and a loop chip's font, and looked
// like one: an artefact of the flow renderer that happened to say "Run test".
//
// `.editor-test-action` and `.active-test-button` are declared here, after the
// borrowed classes, so the flow rules stop deciding what this control looks
// like without any of them having to change.
//
// ## Tokens, not values
//
// Every colour is a `colors-ui-*` mapped token from `styles/generated`. Not
// hygiene: the editor has a light theme and a dark one and the token layer is
// what follows a theme change, so a hex here would be correct in one of them
// and wrong in the other. The sizes are in `em` against the editor's own font
// size, which `makeTestContainer` sets inline per widget — a `px` here would
// stop tracking the editor's zoom.
//
// ## What it must NOT look like
//
// A primary action. There is one of these on every test in the file, and a row
// of filled brand-coloured buttons down the right-hand side of the source would
// compete with the code for attention every time a developer reads the file
// rather than runs it. So it is quiet by default — a hairline outline and a
// label — and only fills in on hover, which is the moment the pointer has
// already declared intent.

.editor-test-action
  display: inline-flex
  align-items: center
  gap: 0.35em

  padding: 0 0.6em
  border: 1px solid colors-ui-border-secondary
  border-radius: 999px

  background-color: transparent
  color: colors-ui-text-primary-label-subtle

  font-family: "SpaceGrotesk"
  font-size: 0.85em
  font-weight: 500
  line-height: 1.6
  white-space: nowrap
  letter-spacing: 0.01em

  cursor: pointer
  user-select: none
  opacity: 0.55
  transition: opacity 120ms ease, background-color 120ms ease, border-color 120ms ease, color 120ms ease

  // THE GLYPH IS A PSEUDO-ELEMENT and not a character in the label, because
  // the label is replaced wholesale by `loadAnimation` while a run is in
  // flight (`el.innerHTML = frames[i]`). A ▶ written into the text node would
  // be erased on the first frame and never come back.
  &:before
    content: "\25b6"
    font-size: 0.75em
    line-height: 1
    opacity: 0.9

  &:hover
    opacity: 1
    background-color: colors-ui-surface-base-card
    border-color: colors-ui-border-primary-hover
    color: colors-ui-text-primary-label

  &:active
    background-color: colors-ui-surface-primary-default-hover

  &:focus-visible
    outline: 2px solid colors-ui-border-focus
    outline-offset: 1px

// RUNNING. Fully opaque and brand-bordered, because at this point the control
// is no longer an affordance offering itself — it is a status, and a status a
// reader has to hover to see is not one.
//
// The animation this class accompanies now STOPS: `loadAnimation` returns when
// the button is no longer the editor's active one, and `settleEditorTestRun`
// is what makes it no longer active. Before that it re-armed a 300 ms timer
// unconditionally and nothing ever cleared `activeTestId`, so a control whose
// message went nowhere on the web build animated for the life of the tab.
.editor-test-action.active-test-button
  opacity: 1
  border-color: colors-ui-border-action
  color: colors-ui-text-primary-body
  background-color: colors-ui-surface-base-card
  cursor: default

  // No pointer affordance while it runs, and no ▶ either: the label is
  // "Running..." and a play triangle beside it would be offering an action
  // that is already happening.
  &:before
    content: ""
    display: none

  &:hover
    background-color: colors-ui-surface-base-card
    border-color: colors-ui-border-action
